When working with Java, you can handle various file types depending on the use case.
Here’s a categorized list:

1. Text-Based Files
.txt (Plain Text)
.csv (Comma-Separated Values)
.log (Log Files)
.json (JavaScript Object Notation)
.xml (Extensible Markup Language)
.yaml / .yml (YAML Ain’t Markup Language)


2. Java-Specific Files
.java (Java Source Code)
.class (Compiled Java Bytecode)
.jar (Java Archive)
.war (Web Application Archive)
.ear (Enterprise Archive)
.properties (Java Properties Configuration)

3. Microsoft Office & Document Files
.docx (Microsoft Word)
.xlsx (Microsoft Excel)
.pptx (Microsoft PowerPoint)
.pdf (Portable Document Format)


4. Database & Serialized Files
.db (Database Files)
.sqlite (SQLite Database)
.sql (SQL Script Files)
.dat (Data Files)
.ser (Serialized Java Objects)


5. Image & Multimedia Files
.jpg / .jpeg (JPEG Image)
.png (Portable Network Graphics)
.gif (Graphics Interchange Format)
.bmp (Bitmap Image)
.tiff (Tagged Image File Format)
.svg (Scalable Vector Graphics)
.mp3 (Audio File)
.wav (Waveform Audio)
.mp4 (MPEG-4 Video)
.avi (Audio Video Interleave)


6. Compressed & Archive Files
.zip (ZIP Archive)
.tar (Tape Archive)
.gz (Gzip Compressed File)
.rar (RAR Archive)


7. Other Special Formats
.ini (Initialization Files)
.cfg (Configuration Files)
.md (Markdown)
.html / .htm (HyperText Markup Language)
.css (Cascading Style Sheets)
.js (JavaScript)


Java provides libraries like java.io, java.nio, java.util.zip,
Gson (for JSON), Apache POI (for Excel/Word), and JSch (for SSH file transfers) to handle these file types.